{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Tzipporah took,</b> etc. On account of the weakness that fell upon Moses, he could not circumcise him, until the matter was revealed to his wife, and she circumcised him." "<b>Tzor.</b> A sharp flint-knife, like 'blades of flint' (חרבות צורים). Another interpretation: <b>tzor</b> in the language of stone, like 'harder than flint' (כשמיר חזק מצור) — for she was on the road and took suddenly whatever she found." "<b>And she touched to his feet.</b> To the child's feet — this is the circumcision, like 'did not make his feet' (לא עשה את רגליו). And one must rearrange the verse: 'And Tzipporah took a flint, and she touched the flint to the child's feet, and cut the foreskin of her son.' Another interpretation: she cast the circumcision at the feet of the angel, to serve in place of an offering, and it was effective — and so we find with Gideon and Manoach, who offered a sacrifice before the angel. Another interpretation: <b>to his feet</b> — to Moses' feet, for through the blood of the mitzvah the plague would depart from him, just as we find in Egypt, where the blood of the Passover prevented the destroyer from entering." "<b>And she said</b> to Moses." "<b>For a bridegroom of blood you are to me.</b> Because of me. What the angel sought to kill you for is because you married into me — for I am a Midianite and you are a Hebrew, and I am not worthy of you."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:56:15.495839492Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}